  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA nearly stationary upper level low centered over the Canadian\nMaritimes will bring cooler air aloft today. Shortwaves rotating\naround the western edge of this low will drop down through\nsouthern Quebec and into northeastern NY/New England later this\nafternoon and evening. These weak features will interact with\nsome residual moisture, which could result in a couple of \nisolated to scattered showers developing in the afternoon, \nmainly over the Catskills and higher elevations of Central NY.\nOtherwise, skies will be partly sunny today and north winds a\nlittle breezy between 7 to 15 mph in the afternoon. Temperatures\nwill be cooler, but near average for early September.\n\n\nThen, for Sunday through Tuesday surface high pressure builds \nin likely keeping the region dry and mostly sunny. Daytime \nhighs will be right around seasonable averages with morning fog \ndeveloping in the valleys. It will be quite cool Sunday night, \nwith good radiational cooling conditions. Lows are forecast to \ndip down into the mid-40s to low 50s in most locations, a few \nareas could be even cooler in the low 40s early Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nUpper level ridging amplifies over the area Tuesday night and\nearly Wednesday. This will bring a deep, moist southwesterly\nflow to the region. Wednesday afternoon into Thursday will have\na deeper shortwave trough progressing through the Northern Tier\nof the US. Ensemble member MSLP means and low pressure centers \nare hinting at a stronger surface low developing and passing \nwell north of the Great Lakes. With most of the ensemble \nmembers keeping the low to the north, not much precipitation is \nanticipated outside of when the cold front moves through. The\nexact timing of the cold front remains uncertain, but is likely\nto be sometime between early Wednesday evening and midday\nThursday. Deterministic models do have a stream of higher PWATs\nand dew points advecting north ahead of the front so depending \non timing of the fronts passage, there could be some stronger \nstorms along it, producing some heavy rain and gusty winds.\nTemperatures look to warm into the 80s on Wednesday out ahead of\nthe front, then cooling back into the 70s on Thursday, and\neventually in the mid-60s to low 70s by Friday for daytime\nhighs. It will be quite humid on Wednesday, with dew points\nlikely to be in the mid-60s to low 70s. Dew points fall through\nthe 60s on Thursday and become much more refreshing in the 50s\nby Friday.",
